  "narrative": "During the 1970s, Black and African women's hairstyles and hair culture were marked by a growing emphasis on natural textures and traditional styles. The afro, a hairstyle created by combing out natural growth of afro-textured hair, was a prominent symbol of black identity and pride. Cornrows, a style of three-strand braids that originated in Africa, became increasingly popular among Black women as a way to adorn their hair with intricate designs. Dreadlocks, also known as dreads or locs, were formed naturally in very curly hair, while box braids emerged as a protective style that added thickness and versatility to natural hair. The Jheri curl, a chemical treatment that gave the wearer a glossy, loosely curled look, was not yet popular during this decade, but its emergence in the 1980s would later influence Black women's hairstyles. While documentation from this era is limited, it is clear that the 1970s laid the groundwork for the natural hair movement, which would resurge in popularity in the 2000s and continue to celebrate the beauty and diversity of Black and African hair textures.",
